		type: 'file',
		src: 'ebooks/lptd/data/cd4/2.mp3',
		subs: [
			{ t: 0.00, e: 3.45, en: 'Unit 3. How the Dinosaurs Disappeared', vi: '' },
			{ t: 4.74, e: 11.80, en: 'The death of the dinosaurs is a great mystery. About 65 million years ago, dinosaurs lived', vi: '' },
			{ t: 12.10, e: 18.48, en: 'all over the Earth. They had existed for nearly 200 million years. Suddenly, they all', vi: '' },
			{ t: 18.78, e: 25.49, en: 'became extinct. Many scientists believe that the dinosaurs were killed by a large meteor.', vi: '' },
			{ t: 25.80, e: 32.86, en: 'They think that this meteor was about 6-12 miles wide. It crashed into southern Mexico and made a', vi: '' },
			{ t: 33.16, e: 40.26, en: 'hole about 130 miles wide. The crashed through dust and dirt into the sky. Dust clouds darkened', vi: '' },
			{ t: 40.56, e: 46.57, en: 'the Earth\'s atmosphere. The crash caused fires, earthquakes, and tidal waves.', vi: '' },
			{ t: 47.18, e: 53.46, en: 'The plants were killed. The oceans were poisoned. Very soon, there was no food left for the', vi: '' },
			{ t: 53.76, e: 59.49, en: 'plant eating dinosaurs. When they died, there was no food for the meat eating dinosaurs.', vi: '' },
			{ t: 59.90, e: 66.96, en: 'The meteor killed about 70% of all plants and animals on Earth. The only animals that could survive', vi: '' },
			{ t: 67.26, e: 73.74, en: 'were small ones that could eat many different kinds of food. Some scientists say the meteor alone', vi: '' },
			{ t: 74.04, e: 80.31, en: 'did not cause dinosaurs to become extinct. They think that dinosaurs were already getting weaker.', vi: '' },
			{ t: 80.96, e: 86.89, en: 'They are not sure why. One reason might be disease. Another might be climate change.', vi: '' },
			{ t: 87.44, e: 94.25, en: 'A big part of the mystery is why some types of animals survived. If climate change killed dinosaurs,', vi: '' },
			{ t: 94.68, e: 100.34, en: 'it should also have killed frogs. If the meteor killed most sea reptiles, it should have killed', vi: '' },
			{ t: 100.64, e: 105.31, en: 'crocodiles. Yet frogs and crocodiles still exist in the world today.', vi: '' },
		]
